>
  <p>
    Vitiligo is the loss of melanocytes, the cells that give skin its colour, producing sharply
    defined white patches. It is autoimmune in origin. It is not contagious, it is not caused by
    anything you ate, and it has nothing to do with hygiene.
  </p>

  <p>
    Medically it is harmless. Socially, in India, it is often not — and that gap between the
    clinical picture and the lived experience is worth naming openly in the consultation rather
    than treating it as a purely cosmetic matter.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    Examination under a Wood's lamp makes patches far clearer, including ones not yet visible to
    the naked eye, and helps distinguish vitiligo from the conditions that mimic it. Sensation is
    tested in the patch — this is how leprosy is excluded, and it takes a moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because vitiligo is autoimmune it associates with other autoimmune conditions, most commonly
    thyroid disease. Thyroid function and, where indicated, other blood tests are checked.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whether the disease is active or stable is the most important judgement at the first visit,
    because it determines whether treatment aims to halt spread or to repigment.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <h3>Topical</h3>
  <p>
    Topical steroids in courses, and calcineurin inhibitors such as tacrolimus — particularly on
    the face and around the eyes, where they avoid steroid-related thinning.
  </p>

  <h3>Phototherapy</h3>
  <p>
    Narrowband UVB is the most effective treatment for widespread vitiligo. It is given as a
    course over months, and response is gradual — small islands of colour appear around hair
    follicles first and slowly coalesce. Patience is part of the treatment.
  </p>

  <h3>Surgical</h3>
  <p>
    For stable disease: melanocyte transfer, or split-thickness skin grafting, into areas that
    have not responded to medical treatment. Only suitable where the condition has not progressed
    for at least a year.
  </p>

  <h3>Camouflage</h3>
  <p>
    Medical camouflage products match skin tone and last through the day. For exposed sites they
    make a substantial difference to daily life while slower treatment continues, and they are
    worth discussing rather than treating as giving up.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sun protection matters more than usual</h3>
    <p>
      Patches have no melanin and burn easily. Sunscreen also prevents surrounding skin from
      tanning, which reduces the contrast and makes patches considerably less conspicuous.
    </p>
  </div>
